course: dictionary
lesson: 15635
title: Tenure: time in a job or a home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tenure is a long word, but context clues help. «During her tenure as head teacher, the school built a new library.» The sentence tells you the head teacher was in charge for a while, and the library was built during that time. So tenure means the length of time someone holds an important job.
explain: Look at the words around it. «After a trial period, Mr Patel was given tenure, so he cannot easily be sacked.» A trial period suggests a test first, and "cannot easily be sacked" tells you the job is kept safe. Here tenure means a permanent job that is hard to take away.
explain: Tenure can also be about a home. «The family had security of tenure, so they could not easily be thrown out of their flat.» Check your meaning by swapping it in: "the right to stay in their flat" still makes sense, so you know you have it right.
fact: Tenure means the length of time someone holds an important job or position.
fact: In the USA, tenure can also mean a permanent job for some teachers and professors, given after a trial period, so they cannot easily be sacked.
fact: Tenure can mean the right to live in or use a house or land, whether you own it (freehold) or rent it.
fact: «Security of tenure» means the right not to be thrown out of your home or job easily.
fact: Tenure is usually uncountable, but you can also say «a tenure», as in «a long tenure in office».
exercise: «During her tenure as head teacher, the school grew.» What does tenure mean here? | The school bell that rang each morning | A new building next door | *The time she held the job | A test she had to pass
exercise: «After a trial period, Mr Patel was given tenure, so he cannot easily be sacked.» What does tenure mean here? | A pay rise for the week | A short job for one term | *A permanent job that is hard to take away | A holiday from school
exercise: «Security of tenure means you cannot easily be thrown out of your home.» What does security of tenure mean? | *The right to stay in your home or job | A party for new neighbours | A form you fill in for the bank | A large garden with a fence
test: «The mayor's tenure in office lasted four years.» What is the best meaning of tenure here? | A quick vote on a new law | *The length of time she held the office | A type of food | The town hall building
test: Which example shows tenure as the right to use a home? | Going on holiday to the seaside | Buying a new bike from the shop | Taking a bus to school each day | *Renting the same flat for years, with the right to stay in it
exam: Lena says, «Tenure is just another word for a job.» Is she right? | Yes, it is a type of food | No, it is a school subject | *No, tenure can mean time in a job, a safe permanent job, or the right to live in a home | Yes, it always means exactly the same as a job
